Who's calling today from eight, three, two.

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:10.180
Hey David, my name is rich.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:14.400
I'm here in Texas and I'm actually running
for the state house of representatives down

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.400
here.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:16.400
All right.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.950
And I'm 28 years old, but young person.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.380
And actually, you know, I just gotta say you're
one of the reasons I got into this kind of

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.960
shit, my policy around listening to you for
so long.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.990
But all that being said, you know, I, you
know, thanks.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:27.990
Yeah.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:29.280
So, um, I do have a question for you.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.860
So, uh, I'll be honest, six months ago, yes,
we should.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.200
Being in Texas, we're being a Democrat.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.860
It's a very, uh, a delicate process.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:39.040
Uh, you know, the idea of Bernie being the
nominee was worrisome.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.970
I'll be honest, and, uh, starting to change
my opinion a lot over the last couple of months.

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:46.690
I mean, even down here in Texas, I think he's
got a good shot at winning, um, the primary,

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:47.730
at the very least.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:52.360
But I will tell you, I mean, places like Fort
bend County, which went blue, uh, for the

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:56.800
first time in decades in 2018 and, uh, actually
was working on a congressional race down here.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.030
So a lot of the folks that voted a Democrat
the first on many years, there were part of

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:02.180
that suburban blue wave.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:07.601
Um, I still, I, you know, I'll be honest with
you, I still had this concern that, uh, Bernie

00:01:07.601 --> 00:01:11.179
being the nominee is gonna maybe not, maybe
not get those folks out to go.

00:01:11.179 --> 00:01:14.189
Maybe they vote back for Trump again, but
I tell you, they came on voting just out of

00:01:14.189 --> 00:01:16.869
their pure disgust, uh, for Donald Trump.

00:01:16.869 --> 00:01:20.439
And while that was good for us in the short
term, I worry longterm, uh, how are we going

00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:21.450
to keep those voters?

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.049
So I'd love to hear your thoughts on that.

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:25.630
And uh, and again, yeah, thank you for doing
what you do.

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:27.189
I've always been a huge fan of you.

00:01:27.189 --> 00:01:29.360
Well, listen, I appreciate the kind words.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:30.360
Here's the thing.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:35.299
If you want to talk about voter turnout, there
is no question that a lot of elections, including

00:01:35.299 --> 00:01:40.030
most likely the one in November, it's going
to hinge not on who changes their mind, but

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:42.229
more on who decides to get out and vote.

00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:50.180
So the question of which candidate will mobilize
versus restrain different groups from choosing

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:55.729
to vote, uh, or, or getting, mobilizing them
to vote or dissuade them from voting?

00:01:55.729 --> 00:02:00.619
It's a very relevant question, but the problem
is if you want to slice and dice it so thin,

00:02:00.619 --> 00:02:04.569
we'll never get anywhere in the, and what
I mean by that is the following.

00:02:04.569 --> 00:02:10.310
It's very plausible that there is a part of
Texas where they don't like Trump, but if

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:13.320
Bernie's the nominee, they just won't vote
or they might vote for Trump.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:14.362
I, I grant that [inaudible] that

00:02:14.362 --> 00:02:19.770
that's probably true, but then there's also
probably a part of Wisconsin where if a centrist

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:23.780
Democrat is the nominee, they'll stay home
even though they don't like Trump, but if

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:26.170
a progressive is the nominee, they will come
out and vote.

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:31.280
So once you start trying to slice and dice
how turn out will be effected in really narrow

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:36.060
areas like part of a state, it becomes an
impossibly complex.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.030
And so my view is we should step back a little
bit.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:44.580
We should at the top level, make sure we're
running a primary that is not so damaging

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:49.660
to the candidates that it will hurt whoever
the eventual nominee is.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:50.660
Right?

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:51.660
That's a top level thing.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:57.690
Number two, let's make sure that the DNC is
not seen as a dishonest broker of the primary,

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.720
which could get people to vote for Trump merely
out of anger or to stay home.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:07.010
So my thinking about turnout is more big picture
than about like, well, what will happen in

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:12.460
Hampton County versus Hampshire County, Massachusetts
if Bernie versus Biden are the nominees?

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:16.070
Because there's too many factors, we can't
possibly account for all of them.

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:17.230
Would that kind of thinking?

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:22.120
Well, I tell you, I probably shouldn't mention,
so the, the, the main concern is, so the Texas

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:24.060
state house is a, I don't know if you're aware
of this.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:28.010
We're only nine seats away from the first
democratic majority in, uh, 18 years.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:32.390
So, uh, if we're able, and we put 12 seats
in 2018, I don't think anybody saw that coming

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:33.390
in these nine seats.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:35.880
They all came very close last time around.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:39.260
So I'm, I'm, I'll be honest, I mean, I, I
think Trump is probably going to win Texas

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:40.540
in November probably.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:41.540
Maybe.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:45.190
But, um, uh, that being said, if you want
to talk about longterm gains be places like

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:49.640
Texas, places like Virginia where they flip
their state house and other States too, where

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.540
we're seeing these margins in the state house
level.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:55.100
And if you want to talk about gerrymandering,
obviously we need concern over the next decade.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.780
I mean, I think, I do think it's something
that we need to be thinking about.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.720
I don't hear enough from Bernie about a down-ballot
momentum.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.390
I hear it from, you know, Joe Biden talks
about the Senate a lot.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:07.180
Obviously it's important we went back to Senate,
but, um, I do think a lot of these state legislatures,

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:10.640
and one of the reasons we're in this mess
is cause you know, folks like the Koch brothers,

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.230
they spend millions of dollars actually not
even that much.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:16.310
I mean low millions over the last 20 years
to flip these state ledge seats.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:20.279
And that's allowed up ballot momentum for
Republicans across the country.

00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:24.629
And I think we really, as Democrats, we need
to be aware that, uh, I really think the state

00:04:24.629 --> 00:04:29.639
houses around the country need to be the key
to at least, uh, providing a fair playing

00:04:29.639 --> 00:04:31.800
field for the next 10 years.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:36.289
No questions, state houses, gerrymandering,
the census, redistricting, a voter suppression,

00:04:36.289 --> 00:04:37.639
all super important stuff.

00:04:37.639 --> 00:04:39.569
And we'll, we'll do our best to keep covering
all of it.

00:04:39.569 --> 00:04:40.689
Rich, I appreciate the call.

00:04:40.689 --> 00:04:42.370
Thank you so much, rich from Texas.

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:43.810
Great to hear from you.

